# Independent Labs pilot/replay spike review (2026-10-22)

**Finding.** The saved giant block medians are recorded elapsed-time regimes, but the original Labs pilot/replay control flow does not provide a specific arithmetic or plan mismatch that explains them. The 512-label and 256-label candidate spikes are both in block 0 (113.293 ms and 60.358 ms medians, about 11.9× and 11.5× their other-block medians), yet both saved plans specify `batch:false` and 20 raw samples. Later blocks replay exactly those choices. Large spikes also occur in nonpilot blocks across candidate and main, so a pilot-only cause cannot explain the full pattern. The source establishes several ways that fresh-process timing can expose transient work or suspension; the saved result does not identify which occurred.

## Pilot versus replay, checked against the original runner

The original worker starts each benchmark block in a fresh Node child, imports the benchmark, runs a short no-op calibration, then measures the selected trial. Block 0 runs with the configured 0.5 s and 20-sample floors; the worker extracts its plan and passes it to blocks 1–7. Blocks are scheduled round-robin across benchmark identities. The replay override fixes `batch`, `batch_samples`, `batch_unroll`, `min_samples`, and `max_samples` from the pilot plan, and sets `min_cpu_time:0` (`labs-original-worker.mjs:55-85,166-185`; `labs-original-src.mjs:699-714`). The CLI converts the configured 0.5 seconds to `5e8` nanoseconds correctly (`dist/cli/cli.mjs:1238-1240`). The named candidate plans are `batch:false`, `batch_samples:0`, `batch_unroll:4`, `samples:20`. Pilot and replay therefore execute the same count of single-call timed samples for these two named runs. The pilot budget could affect other faster runs whose chosen sample count exceeds 20; it is not a tenfold timing multiplier for the two saved spikes.

The original `benchFn` makes one untimed callback invocation, then performs two more only when that first invocation takes at most 500,000 ns (`labs-original-src.mjs:241-280`). Each fresh child can therefore enter the 10–20 ms 512-label callback's timed loop after only one untimed call. The yielded callback alternates text, updates the scene, checks errors, and inspects draws (`benches/labs/package/batching.bench.ts:103-123`). Its state parity and work can matter, but no saved chronological call ledger exists for the original high block. The later trace's stock versus 20-extra-call medians (9.463 versus 8.492 ms pooled) show ordinary warmup sensitivity in that instrumented sitting, with no sample above 50 ms. The trace used the fixed saved plan for every child and omitted a fresh pilot and first-block time rule. It cannot establish that more warmup would have prevented 113 ms.

On Node, Labs uses `performance.now() * 1e6` and saves `t1 - t0` around the callback and result consumption in single-call mode (`labs-original-src.mjs:63-84,389-426`). Forced collections immediately before and after samples contribute to the stopping budget and separate GC summary, not directly to the saved sample duration. GC or runtime work *inside* the callback window and OS descheduling still count in elapsed time. Heap reads and sample-GC instrumentation also occur on each loop, outside the timed callback window. The source shows no conversion, normalization, calibration multiplication, or batching division that could turn an ordinary ~10 ms callback into a ~113 ms saved single-call sample. The file-level and block no-op calibration rates are diagnostics; `mergeStats` pools raw retained nanoseconds unchanged (`labs-original-worker.mjs:121-145`).

Each 20-sample block sorts and removes two lows and two highs. The merger pools 16 survivors per block; the 512 candidate's 128 retained values include 13 above 50 ms and 11 above 100 ms. Its p95 of 117.499 ms is therefore not one untrimmed outlier or a percentile arithmetic mistake. The saved JSON drops raw sample order and block membership of the pooled values, so it cannot show how the high regime evolved within block 0.

## Pattern limits and next observation

The original pattern file covers 145 candidate, 145 main, and 143 published identities. At a threshold of 5× one block versus the median of its other seven, candidate has three hits at index 0 and additional hits at 3 and 5; main has hits at 0, 2, 3, 5, and 6. Published has one at 0. At 2×, published clusters at late indices 6 and 7 (15 and 13 identities). Thus the data supports multiple block-local timing regimes and some sitting-wide clustering, not a universal first-block anomaly. These files cover this Glyph matrix only; they cannot establish a cause common to other repositories. Keep all original reports and outliers.

If a giant regime recurs in the already-running fixed-warmup matrix or a later authorized diagnostic, the smallest decisive observation is a per-child chronological ledger joined to each raw Labs `t0/t1` sample: callback wall interval and call index, process CPU/context-switch deltas, GC/optimization timestamps, and block/pilot identity. Preserve the original trim and sample arithmetic. A high Labs interval without a high callback interval points toward runner/consume timing; high callback wall with little attributable CPU and scheduler evidence points toward suspension; high callback wall and CPU with aligned V8 events points toward runtime or actual callback work. Process-wide CPU/context counters alone are insufficient attribution. The existing trace provides the alignment design but had no recurrence, so it cannot decide among these mechanisms. The current uniform fixed-20 option addresses the demonstrated short-warmup weakness; evaluate its matrix as a new protocol, not as a proven repair of the historical 113 ms block.

